@Introduction

<Paragraph>
    In the <LinkA Href="https://mlir.llvm.org/docs/Tutorials/Toy/Ch-1/">first chapter of the MLIR Toy Tutorial</LinkA>
    a recursive-descent parser is implemented. It would be a very boring chapter if I redid that &ndash; I wrote like
    a dozen recursive-descent parsers in my life already.<Footnote>You can find a good intro to lexing and parsing
        in the excellent (and free!) <LinkA Href="https://craftinginterpreters.com">Crafting Interpreters</LinkA>
        book.</Footnote> Instead, I will use <LinkA Href="https://github.com/lalrpop/lalrpop/">lalrpop</LinkA>, a Rust
    crate for generating LR(1) parsers.<Footnote>The name suggests LALR(1), but the LALR(1) backend
        is no longer available in newest lalrpop. I'm assuming they had a good reason for it.</Footnote>
</Paragraph>
<Paragraph GutterBottom="false">
    All of the code for this tutorial is published in
    <LinkA Href="https://github.com/V0ldek/mlir-toy-rust">the mlir-toy-rust GitHub repository</LinkA>.
    The code for this chapter is on the <Code>ch1</Code> branch.
</Paragraph>

<Header2>The Toy language</Header2>

<Paragraph>
    Toy is a tensor-based language. The word &ldquo;tensor&rdquo; has precise meaning in mathematics, but for some reason computer
    scientists abuse that word to mean &ldquo;multi-dimensional&rdquo; array.<Footnote>
        I'm not sure how and why this happened. Tensors are general mathematical objects. They can be represented
        as a multi-dimensional matrix if you fix some basis, but if we're talking about that then it's not a tensor
        &ndash; it's a matrix. The only reason to use the precise term &ldquo;tensor&rdquo; is if you don't want to talk about
        a single particular corresponding matrix, so why is it used here to refer to a concrete array of numbers?
        Beats me. On the other hand, Google called their chips &ldquo;Tensor Processing Units&rdquo;, so I think I'm way too late
        to win this fight.
    </Footnote>
    Wherever you see the word &ldquo;tensor&rdquo; going forward,
    just substitute &ldquo;array&rdquo; instead and you'll be right. However, MLIR uses the word &ldquo;tensor&rdquo;
    internally for types of values, so I'll be using it as well to avoid confusion.
</Paragraph>
<Paragraph>
    The language allows any-dimensional tensors, but the tutorial only implements codegen for $1$ and $2$-dimensional
    ones. All numerical values are $64$-bit floating point values (double-precision). All values are immutable.
    We can define functions, perform basic operations on tensors (addition and element-wise multiplication),
    and use a builtin <Code>print</Code> function to print results. Here's example Toy code (comments are given by <Code>#</Code>):
</Paragraph>
<CodeBlock Language="python" LanguageString="Toy" LineNumbers="true" Code="@(@"
def main() {
  # Define a variable `a` with shape <2, 3>, initialized with the literal value.
  # The shape is inferred from the supplied literal.
  var a = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]];

  # b is identical to a, the literal tensor is implicitly reshaped: defining new
  # variables is the way to reshape tensors (element count must match).
  var b<2, 3> = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

  # transpose() and print() are the only builtin, the following will transpose
  # a and b and perform an element-wise multiplication before printing the result.
  print(transpose(a) * transpose(b));
}")"></CodeBlock>
<Paragraph>
    All tensors are statically typed. The user <em>may</em> specify the type of a declared variable,
    but function arguments and return types are implicitly typed. Functions are effectively generic,
    and they are specialised to the specific tensor shapes they are called with.
</Paragraph>
<CodeBlock Language="python" LanguageString="Toy" LineNumbers="true" Code="@(@"
# User defined generic function that operates on unknown shaped arguments.
def multiply_transpose(a, b) {
  return transpose(a) * transpose(b);
}

def main() {
  # Define a variable `a` with shape <2, 3>, initialized with the literal value.
  var a = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]];
  var b<2, 3> = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];

  # This call will specialize `multiply_transpose` with <2, 3> for both
  # arguments and deduce a return type of <3, 2> in initialization of `c`.
  var c = multiply_transpose(a, b);

  # A second call to `multiply_transpose` with <2, 3> for both arguments will
  # reuse the previously specialized and inferred version and return <3, 2>.
  var d = multiply_transpose(b, a);

  # A new call with <3, 2> (instead of <2, 3>) for both dimensions will
  # trigger another specialization of `multiply_transpose`.
  var e = multiply_transpose(c, d);

  # Finally, calling into `multiply_transpose` with incompatible shapes
  # (<2, 3> and <3, 2>) will trigger a shape inference error.
  var f = multiply_transpose(a, c);
}")"></CodeBlock>
<Paragraph>
    We need a parser to convert this textual representation to an Abstract Syntax Tree.
    We diverge from the original tutorial in two ways: first, I will be using <Code>lalrpop</Code> as mentioned
    in the intro; second, I split the <Code>Expr</Code> node type to distinguish between top-level statements
    that can occur in a function's body, and generic expressions that can occur e.g. as a side of a binary operator.
    With those caveats, let's take an example that has all syntax elements and translate it to an AST:
</Paragraph>

<MudContainer Class="d-flex" Gutters="false">
    <MudContainer Gutters="false" Class="mr-1">
        <CodeBlock Language="python" LanguageString="Toy" LineNumbers="true" Code="@(@"
# Comments are ignored.
def multiply_transpose(a, b) {
  return transpose(a) * transpose(b);
}

def main() {
  var a = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]];
  var b<2, 3> = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6];
  var c = multiply_transpose(a, b);
  print(c + c);
  return;
}")"></CodeBlock>
        <MudPaper Class="mr-3 mb-3 pa-3 d-flex align-center justify-center mud-width-full" Elevation="0">
            <ThemedImage Alt="diagram visualising the xor trick"
                         Title="The XOR trick"
                         Src="img/sorcery/making-languages-in-rust/sourcery-mlir-toy-ast.svg"
                         Fluid="true"
                         Width="400"
                         Height="400"/>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudContainer>
    <MudContainer Gutters="false" Class="ml-1">
        <CodeBlock Language="plain" LanguageString="Plain text" Code="@(@"
Module @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@1:1
|--Function multiply_transpose [args = (a, b)] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@2:1
|  |--Return @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:3
|  |  |--BinOp [op = Multiply] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:10
|  |  |  |--Call [callee = transpose] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:10
|  |  |  |  |--Var [ident = a] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:20
|  |  |  |--Call [callee = transpose] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:25
|  |  |  |  |--Var [ident = b] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@3:35
|--Function main [args = ()] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@6:1
|  |--VarDecl [ident = a] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@7:3
|  |  |--Literal [ty = <2, 3>] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@7:11
|  |  |  |--Literal [ty = <3>] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@7:12
|  |  |  |  |--Num [value = 1] @./toy/valid/ch1.toy@7:13
